<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      allowBackup is off, and that is a decision rather than a default.

      Android's auto-backup copies an application's data directory to the
      account holder's Drive. For FamilyOS that directory is the WebView's
      IndexedDB: a household's records, and the wrapped key material beside
      them. The wrapping is sound, and it is only as strong as what wraps it —
      `auth/lock.js` allows a PIN of four digits, so ten thousand candidates
      stand between an exfiltrated store and the records in it. Rate limiting
      guards the keypad, not a copy of the file. An application whose premise
      is "encrypted, on this device" should not put that copy on somebody
      else's server by default.

      READ THIS BEFORE SHIPPING. What it costs is not small, and an earlier
      version of this comment got it wrong by saying the recovery phrase and
      Drive sync cover it. They do not, here. The recovery phrase restores a
      *key*, not data — Settings says so in as many words — and Drive sync
      needs Google sign-in, which does not work inside a WebView (see
      docs/CAPACITOR_SETUP.md). So on a native build today, with this off,
      a lost or wiped phone means the records are gone.

      That is a real trade-off between a weak-PIN exposure and losing
      everything, and it belongs to the household rather than to this file.
      It is off because the exposure is silent and the loss is not: nobody is
      told their key material went to Drive, and everybody notices a lost
      phone. Turn it back on by changing `false` to `true` below; nothing else
      depends on it.

      usesCleartextTraffic is stated rather than inherited. It is already the
      platform default above API 27, and writing it down means a later
      targetSdk change cannot silently flip it.
    -->
    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode|navigation|density"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <!--
          The location trail's foreground service. `exported="false"` because
          nothing outside this application has any business starting it, and
          the location type is required from Android 14 for a service that
          reads a position.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".LocationTrailService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="location" />

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!-- Permissions -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
        Location, for Phase 15: safe zones and the "where is everyone" screen.
        Both are asked for at runtime by @capacitor/geolocation, and the app
        works without them — every screen degrades to "no reading on this
        device" rather than breaking.

        COARSE is listed as well as FINE deliberately. Android lets a person
        grant the approximate one only, and a household that would rather say
        "the right neighbourhood" than "the right doorstep" should be able to:
        a coarse fix still decides a 3 km zone, and `domain/geo.js` refuses to
        place it against a 200 m one instead of guessing.

        ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION *is* now here, above, and the paragraph
        that used to stand in this place said its absence was the design. That
        changed on a deliberate decision, not by accident — everything that
        asserted the opposite was rewritten in the same change rather than
        left to contradict the manifest. docs/LOCATION.md carries what was
        given up and what was gained.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
        Declared not-required so the Play listing does not exclude devices
        without GPS hardware. The feature is optional here in the real sense:
        a device that cannot fix a position gets a screen that says so.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.location.gps" android:required="false" />


    <!--
      Background location, added deliberately and against the advice recorded
      in the previous version of this file. It is what makes a safe zone mean
      anything when the phone is in a pocket, which is the only state a person
      actually cares about being told they crossed one in.

      What it costs, written down rather than discovered:

        * A foreground service with a notification the person cannot dismiss
          while it runs. `LocationTrailService` provides one. Android requires
          it and so does honesty — a phone recording positions should say so
          on its own screen.
        * On Android 10+ this is a SEPARATE grant from the foreground ones,
          which the system will not show in the same prompt. The person has to
          choose "Allow all the time" in system settings, and
          `BackgroundLocationPlugin` sends them there rather than pretending a
          runtime request can do it.
        * A Play Store background-location declaration and a demonstration
          video. That is true of both flavours: the `sms` one already cannot
          go to Play because of READ_SMS, and `standard` — which drops it so
          the app installs at all — would still need the declaration for
          this.

      OFF BY DEFAULT AND STAYS OFF until somebody turns it on. Nothing starts
      the service at boot, nothing starts it on launch, and a test asserts
      both. See docs/LOCATION.md.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      The foreground service's notification. On Android 13+ posting one needs
      a runtime grant, so without this the service starts and is invisible —
      which is the one thing a background recorder must never be.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      Screen time. PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS is a *special access* permission: it
      cannot be requested at runtime and the system will not prompt for it.
      A person has to grant it in Settings, and `ScreenTimePlugin` opens that
      page rather than failing silently. `tools:ignore` because the linter
      flags it as not-runtime-grantable, which is the point.

      Gated by consent, not by the permission alone. Reading which
      applications somebody used is a record *about that person*, so
      `js/services/screentime.js` refuses unless `js/data/consent.js` holds a
      decision for them. The grant is necessary and not sufficient.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.PACKAGE_USAGE_STATS"
        tools:ignore="ProtectedPermissions" />

</manifest>
